---
/**
 * What the site logs about readers. Keep this in step with the header of
 * analytics/visit.js and the README section "Visitor analytics"; the three say
 * the same things.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Privacy · Akshat Valse';
const description = 'What akshatvalse.com records about the pages that are read, what it never stores, and how to opt out.';
const CODE = 'https://github.com/AkshatValse/akshatvalse-site/blob/main/analytics/visit.js';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Privacy', path: '/privacy' }]}>
  <div class="container page" id="first-content">
    <div class="page-title">
      <h1 class="title">Privacy</h1>
      <p>
        This site counts which pages are read and from which networks. It does not identify readers.
        Here is exactly what that means.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="about-grid">
      <div class="about-label"><p>01&nbsp;/&nbsp;What is recorded</p></div>
      <div class="about-body">
        <p>When a page is requested, the site adds one row to a small database. A row holds:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>the hour of the request (not the minute) and the page's path;</li>
          <li>the country, and the name and number of the network the request came from: a university, a company, or a home or mobile provider;</li>
          <li>a short label for where the reader came from, such as <code>google</code>, <code>linkedin</code>, a university's domain, or this site, in which case also the page the reader was on;</li>
          <li>whether the device is a phone or a desktop;</li>
          <li>whether the request came from a person or an automated client, and for an automated one its kind: a link preview, a search or AI crawler, an AI assistant, a monitor, or a script.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          When a page is hidden or closed, a small script sends one more row: the same network details,
          how long the page was visible, in buckets (under 10 seconds, 10 to 30 seconds, and so on), and
          how far it was scrolled, in quarters. Downloading the CV is recorded like a page view.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-label"><p>02&nbsp;/&nbsp;What is never stored</p></div>
      <div class="about-body">
        <p>
          IP addresses, or anything made from one; cookies or any other identifier of a reader;
          user-agent strings; full referrer addresses; query strings; anything that links one visit to
          the next. The only cookie this site ever sets is one I set in my own browsers so that my own
          visits are not counted.
        </p>
        <p>
          A row has no identifier, but on a very small network a few rows in the same hour may be one
          person. I treat the data that way, and I read it in a terminal, not on a public page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-label"><p>03&nbsp;/&nbsp;Opting out</p></div>
      <div class="about-body">
        <p>
          Nothing is recorded for a request that carries Global Privacy Control (<code>Sec-GPC: 1</code>)
          or Do Not Track (<code>DNT: 1</code>). Turning either on in your browser is enough, and it
          works in private windows too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-label"><p>04&nbsp;/&nbsp;How long</p></div>
      <div class="about-body">
        <p>Rows are deleted after 400 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="about-label"><p>05&nbsp;/&nbsp;Hosting and code</p></div>
      <div class="about-body">
        <p>
          The site is served by Cloudflare Pages. As the host, Cloudflare sees the IP address of every
          request, as any host does; I never receive it. Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is switched
          on, counts page views, referrers, countries and devices without cookies. The code that writes
          the rows is public: <a href={CODE}><code>analytics/visit.js</code></a> lists every field and
          is the authority if this page and the code ever differ.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
